# Copy to .env and adjust. Nothing in the repository reads a default for these.
#
# **This file is the development template.** A deployment's .env is the same file with production
# values, held on the box at mode 0600 and never in the repository — see README.md, "Deployment".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One switch: no external provider is reached at all.
#
# `true` puts transcription, generation and mail on their fakes together — it overrides
# ASR_PROVIDER, GENERATE_PROVIDER and MAIL_TRANSPORT below, deliberately, because a switch with
# exceptions in it cannot promise that nothing leaves the machine.
#
# **It is not a convenience.** Development runs against the MinIO container, and the worker hands
# the ASR provider a signed URL to fetch the object *itself* rather than sending it bytes — a
# bucket on 127.0.0.1 is not reachable from Deepgram, so a MinIO development environment cannot use
# real transcription at all. This is the honest statement of that.
#
# Leave it on unless you specifically mean to spend money. A deployment never sets it, and
# `npm run verify:production` asserts that on the box.
#
# Reaching a real provider from development therefore means: set this to `false`, point the MEDIA_
# block at a real bucket, and use a **second, development-only** API key for each provider — never
# production's, or the bill stops being able to tell you what production cost.
THP_MOCK_EXTERNAL=true

# Postgres. The instance must have the `vector` extension AVAILABLE (installed as a package) and
# NOT enabled — this epic asserts both. See README.md, "Database".
DATABASE_URL=postgres://thp:thp@127.0.0.1:5432/thp

# The absolute origin the client calls. There is no same-host fallback by design: the packaged
# mobile build later changes this one value and nothing else.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# The first admin, created by `npm run seed:admin`. The command refuses a weak or absent password
# rather than seeding a guessable account, and running it again leaves an existing account — and its
# password — untouched. Not read anywhere else.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
SEED_ADMIN_DISPLAY_NAME=First Admin
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Transactional email. Two messages so far: the invitation and the password-reset link. Both go
# through one adapter, so adding a third is a template rather than a configuration change.
#
# The adapter speaks SMTP and names no vendor, so moving between Resend, Postmark, a Fastmail
# mailbox and a self-hosted relay is these four values rather than a change of code. The values
# below are Resend's, which is what this deployment sends through: the user is the literal word
# `resend` and the password is an API key from https://resend.com/api-keys.
#
# `smtp` is the only transport a deployment should ever name. `capture` writes each message to
# MAIL_CAPTURE_PATH as JSON lines instead of sending it — that is what `npm test` uses, and what
# lets you open the real rendered invitation in a browser during development. `failing` refuses
# everything and exists only so the suite can drive a send failure.
#
# THP_MOCK_EXTERNAL=true forces `capture` regardless of what this says — capture rather than
# failing, so a mocked environment still lets you open the rendered invitation.
MAIL_TRANSPORT=smtp
MAIL_HOST=smtp.resend.com
MAIL_PORT=465
MAIL_USER=resend
MAIL_PASSWORD=
# The sender every message goes out as. The domain must be one you have verified with the
# provider, or the mail will be rejected or filed as spam — see README.md, "Email".
MAIL_FROM=Teaching Hub <invitations@example.com>
# Only read when MAIL_TRANSPORT=capture. Keep it under .tmp/, which is gitignored: a captured
# invitation or reset message contains a working token.
MAIL_CAPTURE_PATH=.tmp/mail/outbox.jsonl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The media store. Original audio uploads, and nothing else — no processed rendition exists.
#
# The adapter speaks plain S3 and names no vendor, so moving between Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2,
# AWS S3 and the MinIO container development runs against is these five values rather than a change
# of code. The values below are the container's (`docker compose up -d minio`); a deployment
# replaces all five with its bucket's. See README.md, "Media store".
#
# **The test suite does not read these.** It talks only to the MinIO container, with the container's
# own credentials held in tests/setup/media-bucket.ts — because these five may point at a real
# bucket, and a bucket this product can never delete from is not somewhere a test run may write.
#
# Two properties of the real bucket are set outside this file and are expensive to discover late:
# it must **not be publicly readable**, and it must carry a **CORS rule permitting PUT and its
# preflight from the application origin, with `content-type` on the allowed headers** — without
# that rule the browser cannot make the upload at all.
#
# Nothing is ever deleted from this bucket. The original is the input transcription reads and every
# later re-transcription depends on, and the media store has no delete operation to call.
MEDIA_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:9000
# R2 wants `auto`; a real S3 region works unchanged.
MEDIA_REGION=auto
MEDIA_BUCKET=thp-media
MEDIA_ACCESS_KEY_ID=thp
MEDIA_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=thp-secret-not-a-real-key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Speech to text. One adapter, one provider behind it, named here rather than in code — swapping is
# this block plus one file (tests/guards/asr-boundary.test.ts is what keeps that true).
#
# `deepgram` is the real one and the only one a deployment should ever name. Nova-3 pre-recorded,
# monolingual English, at $0.0043/min = $0.258/hr — which is the line
# docs/project/architecture.md § Estimated running costs is built on. A 90-minute teaching is ~$0.39.
#
# `fake` reads a fixed script off ASR_FAKE_SCRIPT and returns it, spending nothing and reaching no
# network. That is what `npm test` runs against, and what lets you drive the whole pipeline end to
# end with no account. THP_MOCK_EXTERNAL=true forces it regardless of what this says.
ASR_PROVIDER=deepgram
# A Member-scoped key from https://console.deepgram.com. No default: a missing key fails at the
# first transcription naming this variable, not as an authorisation error inside a provider call.
ASR_API_KEY=
# Only read when ASR_PROVIDER=fake. A JSON file holding { language, confidence, durationSeconds,
# segments: [{ startMs, endMs, text }] }.
ASR_FAKE_SCRIPT=packages/worker/tests/fixtures/teaching-script.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Drafting the summary and the description. One adapter, one provider behind it, named here rather
# than in code — swapping is this block plus one file (tests/guards/generate-boundary.test.ts is
# what keeps that true).
#
# `minimax` is the real one and the only one a deployment should ever name. MiniMax M3 over the
# Anthropic-compatible endpoint, at $0.30/M input and $1.20/M output below 512K input — an
# ~80k-token transcript plus ~1k of output is about $0.025 a recording, against the $2 LLM line in
# docs/project/architecture.md § Estimated running costs. The two artefacts come back as one forced
# tool call; a model that answers in prose fails the job, which reads on /admin/pipeline and is
# re-runnable from it.
#
# **The whole transcript of every teaching leaves the system to this provider**, which is outside
# the EU. Third-party LLM egress was already the architecture's position; the jurisdiction is the
# operator's settled decision.
#
# `fake` reads a fixed script off GENERATE_FAKE_SCRIPT and returns it, spending nothing and
# reaching no network. That is what `npm test` runs against, and what lets you drive the whole
# pipeline end to end with no account. THP_MOCK_EXTERNAL=true forces it regardless of what this says.
GENERATE_PROVIDER=minimax
# A key from the international platform, https://platform.minimax.io. No default: a missing key
# fails at the first generation naming this variable, not as an authorisation error inside a
# provider call.
GENERATE_API_KEY=
# Only read when GENERATE_PROVIDER=fake. A JSON file holding { summary, description } — the same
# field names the tool call carries.
GENERATE_FAKE_SCRIPT=packages/worker/tests/fixtures/draft-script.json
